A lookup takes the slot just past a table as one of its slots.
The first bytes it reads are the table's padding, which pool_alloc zeroed,
so the slot reads as empty and the call fails as it should:
nothing the kernel does shows the read,
which reaches outside the table all the same.
The seed slot-past-table names that slot, as the capability a call invokes and as a copy's source.
Memory safety.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-smp2 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: the kernel reached RAM outside its objects

diff --git a/kernel/cap.c b/kernel/cap.c
--- a/kernel/cap.c
+++ b/kernel/cap.c
@@ -9,7 +9,7 @@
 
 int cap_lookup(struct captable *table, uint32_t slot, struct cap *out)
 {
-    if (slot >= table->nslots) {
+    if (slot > table->nslots) {
         return KERR_INVALID_CAP;
     }
     struct cap c = table->slots[slot];
